	// proposeEditDescription is propose edit description.
	proposeEditDescription = "Propose edits to the document. Each lands as the user set it - a revision they accept or reject, or written straight in (their message says so). Every edit of the reply goes in this one call's `edits`, one item per distinct edit, in document order. For each, pick the action that matches the intent: `replace` swaps `anchor` for `text`; `replace_selection` swaps the user's currently selected text (the <selected-text> block in their message; no `anchor` needed) for `text`; `insert_before`/`insert_after` add `text` around an untouched `anchor` - inside the paragraph when the anchor is a phrase or sentence within it (write `text` as words that read on from it - after a paragraph's last sentence, this continues the paragraph), as a new paragraph when the anchor is a whole block or `text` is block content (a heading, list, several paragraphs), and as a new line of the same table or list when the anchor is a whole row or list item line and `text` is another one; `delete` removes `anchor`; `append` adds `text` at the end of the document; `insert_at_cursor` adds `text` exactly where the user's cursor is (only when their message carries a <cursor> block showing it; no `anchor` needed). Name the target by its block: every block of the document (a paragraph, a heading, a whole list, a quote, a table, a code block, a picture) starts with its id, like `[#k3f9]`, and `block` takes that id - write it first, before anything else, so the user sees where the edit goes at once. With `block` alone, `replace` swaps the whole block for `text`, `delete` removes it, and `insert_before`/`insert_after` add `text` as a new block next to it. To change a few words inside a block, give `block` and, as `anchor`, just those words quoted from it - not the whole block; to add text inside a block (after a sentence, or a list item of a list), give `block` and, as `anchor`, the words or the item line it goes next to. To rewrite a run of consecutive blocks in one replace - however long: a section, the whole document - give the first as `block` and the last as `last_block`, and the run rewritten as `text`. The user still decides on each paragraph by itself. Pictures in the run stay as they are, written in `text` as they were: a text edit never changes or removes a picture. When the user asks for a picture to change too (its people, its scene, its style), redo it with generate_image by its block first, then propose the text, in the same reply - writing that you changed it is not changing it. `delete` by its block removes one. The ids aren't part of the text: never write them in `text` or `anchor`. Words quoted in `anchor` must occur exactly once in the block - quote a few more if they repeat. Without `block` is only for text you proposed earlier that the user hasn't decided on, which has no id: then `anchor` quotes it verbatim, once."
	// findFilesDescription is find files description.
	findFilesDescription = "Find files in the project folder by name - text files, and PDF, Word, PowerPoint, spreadsheet, EPUB and RTF files (case-insensitive; every word must appear in the path, so \"chapter 3\" finds drafts/chapter-3.md). Markdown and plain-text documents come first. Leave `name` out to list every file, to see what reference material there is; then read_file."
